WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.347
Today you add big numbers, take
them away, and pay for things.

2
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:12.608
You will make a hundred, carry
a ten, and count out change.

3
00:00:13.108 --> 00:00:16.266
A complement is what you still
need to reach a round number.

4
00:00:16.486 --> 00:00:19.690
You have 62. How many more make 100?

5
00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:23.904
Count on to the next ten
first. 62 to 70 is 8.

6
00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:28.954
Then 70 to 100 is 30.
And 8 and 30 make 38.

7
00:00:29.174 --> 00:00:33.957
So 62 and 38 are complements.
62 add 38 is 100.

8
00:00:34.457 --> 00:00:40.355
Adding can be done in any order
you like. Look at 17 add 9 add 3.

9
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:44.986
Start with 17 and 3. They make
20, which is a round number.

10
00:00:45.206 --> 00:00:49.665
Now 20 add 9 is 29. Choosing
the order made the sum easy.

11
00:00:49.885 --> 00:00:55.086
Taking away is not like this. 10
take away 3 is not 3 take away 10.

12
00:00:55.586 --> 00:00:58.883
Add one column at a time,
starting from the right.

13
00:00:59.103 --> 00:01:04.212
247 add 185. The ones
first: 7 and 5 make 12.

14
00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:08.379
12 is too big for the ones.
Write 2, and carry the ten.

15
00:01:08.599 --> 00:01:14.822
The tens: 4 and 8 and the carried
1 make 13. Write 3, carry again.

16
00:01:15.042 --> 00:01:21.126
The hundreds: 2 and 1 and 1
make 4. So the answer is 432.

17
00:01:21.346 --> 00:01:27.011
Check it roughly: 200 add 200
is about 400. 432 is close.

18
00:01:27.511 --> 00:01:31.041
Taking away goes down the
same columns, from the right.

19
00:01:31.261 --> 00:01:36.741
400 take away 178. In the
ones, 0 is too small for 8.

20
00:01:36.961 --> 00:01:40.444
Never swap the digits round.
Fetch a ten from the left.

21
00:01:40.664 --> 00:01:46.654
The hundreds lend one. 4 becomes
3, 0 becomes 9, and 0 becomes 10.

22
00:01:46.874 --> 00:01:51.518
Now take away down the columns:
2, 2 and 2. That is 222.

23
00:01:52.018 --> 00:01:56.198
An amount of money has a dot in
it. Look at four point six five.

24
00:01:56.418 --> 00:02:00.087
The four is whole shillings.
The six and the five are cents.

25
00:02:00.307 --> 00:02:05.044
There are 100 cents in a shilling,
not ten. Read both figures.

26
00:02:05.264 --> 00:02:08.979
So three point zero five is
three shillings and five cents.

27
00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:13.750
Not fifty cents. The zero is
holding the ten cents column open.

28
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.547
Change is the gap between
the price and what you paid.

29
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:21.714
It costs two shillings and sixty
cents. You pay five shillings.

30
00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:26.068
Count on. From two sixty up to
three shillings is forty cents.

31
00:02:26.288 --> 00:02:29.631
Then three shillings up to
five is two more shillings.

32
00:02:29.851 --> 00:02:32.638
So the change is two
shillings and forty cents.

33
00:02:33.138 --> 00:02:36.714
You can find a complement to
100, and add in any order.

34
00:02:36.934 --> 00:02:41.438
You carry when a column is too big,
and exchange when it is too small.

35
00:02:41.658 --> 00:02:45.745
You read both figures after the
dot, and count on to find change.
